+## Bounded Staleness
+
+{{< public-preview />}}
+
+Bounded Staleness provides the guarantees of [Serializable](#serializable)
+while also bounding how stale the data returned by a query can be. You specify
+the bound as a duration `D`; queries are then served at the freshest timestamp
+`T` that satisfies `oracle.read_ts(now) - T <= D`, without waiting for input
+frontiers to advance.
+
+This is useful when you want data that is "no older than `D`" but do not want
+to pay the latency of waiting for the freshest possible timestamp, as [Strict
+Serializable](#strict-serializable) does.
+
+### Restrictions
+
+-   Bounded Staleness is read-only. `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, and `COPY
+    FROM` are rejected in a session using this isolation level.
+-   Bounded Staleness cannot be combined with [real-time
+    recency](#real-time-recency). Setting `real_time_recency = on` in a session
+    using this isolation level is rejected.
+-   Bounded Staleness is only supported on the wall-clock
+    (`EpochMilliseconds`) timeline. Queries that touch other timelines are
+    rejected.
+-   The duration `D` must be greater than zero. There is no upper bound on
+    `D`.
+
+### Errors
+
+If no timestamp within the bound can be served without waiting, the query
+fails with a `BoundedStalenessExceeded` error (SQLSTATE `40001`), which
+includes the size of the gap and, when available, the slowest input.
+
+### Enabling Bounded Staleness
+
+Bounded Staleness is gated behind the `enable_bounded_staleness_isolation`
+[feature flag](/reference/feature-flags/). Once enabled, set the isolation
+level per session, for example:
+
+```mzsql
+SET TRANSACTION_ISOLATION TO 'bounded staleness 5s';
+```
+
+The duration accepts [interval](/sql/types/interval/) values such as `500ms`,
+`5s`, or `1m30s`.
+
